Notebook[{
Cell["PadicSignature", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"1e6c38b1-16e2-485d-87ca-0751a7943b9b"],

Cell["\<\
PadicSignature[r,p] gives {n,k} for the exact expansion of the normalized \
rational unit: n stored digits, k repeating digits, and n-k nonrepeating \
digits. Zero has signature {0,0}; terminating expansions have k=0. \
PadicSignature[s] uses a PadicRational object's base; PadicSignature[s,p] \
uses prime p. The period is determined without enumerating its digits. Lazy \
and finite objects have no direct signature form; use \
PadicSignature[Normal[s],p] for a lazy value.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"d0ee26e9-bf83-432b-97b1-70aa2a05438c"],

Cell["\<\
The signature describes the normalized unit, excluding valuation-related zero \
padding. The n-k nonrepeating digits occur at the lowest places, before \
repetition begins toward higher powers.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"b9690053-2b5a-451f-92cd-d38dfd4b2800"],

Cell["\<\
The period length is found number-theoretically without storing its digit \
cycle. This query accepts exact integers, rationals, and exact p-adic objects.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"dcc4c602-4aef-46da-be3d-e0e61bb3d5a8"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"6f230f39-8834-4802-8d62-\
7881409c6057"],

